---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import InstallLine from '../components/InstallLine.astro';
import { FUNCTIONS, CODE_FUNCTIONS, SURFACES, HOWTOS, FACTS } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { cell, SURFACE_SAMPLES } from '../data/examples.mjs';

const tabs = [
  { label: 'Bash', alias: 'Bash', surface: 'shell' },
  { label: 'Python', alias: 'Python', surface: 'python' },
  { label: 'TypeScript', alias: 'TypeScript', surface: 'typescript' },
  { label: 'Ruby', alias: 'Ruby', surface: 'ruby' },
  { label: 'R', alias: 'R', surface: 'r' },
  { label: 'Rust', alias: 'Rust', surface: 'rust' },
  { label: 'SQL', alias: 'SQL', surface: 'duckdb' },
];

const bash = [cell('filter', 'shell').runs[0]];
const surfaceOf = (slug) => SURFACES.find((s) => s.slug === slug);
const plannedCaption = (slug) => surfaceOf(slug).name + ' is planned. This is how the calls will read.';
const sixHowtos = HOWTOS.slice(0, 6);
---
<Base title="ThinkThen">
  <h1>Code that understands.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Your code can branch on an exit code, filter with a pattern, and sort by a column. It cannot branch on what a message means. So the messy cases go to a person, or to a regex that breaks by Friday, or to a chat model wrapped in a prompt, a parser, a retry loop, and a bill.</p>

  <p>ThinkThen gives your code ten functions that understand. <code>decide</code> is an if that reads. <code>filter</code> is a grep that reads. <code>rank</code> is a sort that reads. <code>choose</code>, <code>tag</code>, <code>score</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>annotate</code>, <code>recognize</code>, and <code>relate</code> cover the rest. Each one asks one bounded question about a piece of evidence and hands back a plain answer with a probability. You set the bar. Anything in the middle comes back as not sure, and your code sends it to a person.</p>

  <div class="tabbed" data-fallback="Bash">
    <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
      {tabs.map((t) => (
        <button type="button" role="tab" data-lang={t.label} data-alias={t.alias} aria-selected={t.label === 'Bash'}>{t.label}</button>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="cell" data-lang="Bash">
      {bash.map((run) => <Terminal run={run} caption="Run against the shipped command and saved." />)}
      <InstallLine line={surfaceOf('shell').install[0][0]} status="planned" note="coming with 0.1" caption="Install the command" />
    </div>
    {tabs.slice(1).map((t) => (
      <div class="cell" data-lang={t.label} hidden>
        <Code code={SURFACE_SAMPLES[t.surface].code} caption={plannedCaption(t.surface)} />
        <InstallLine line={surfaceOf(t.surface).install[0][0]} status="planned" caption="Install, once it ships" />
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/install/">Every way to install it</a> · <a href="/surfaces/">The same ten functions in Bash, Python, TypeScript, Ruby, R, Rust, C, and SQL</a></p>

  <h2>The ten functions</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {CODE_FUNCTIONS.map((fn) => (
      <a class="tile" href={`/functions/${fn.name}/`}>
        <b>{fn.name}</b>
        <span>{fn.line}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/functions/question-file/">A question is also a small file</a> · <a href="/tutorial/">The tutorial</a></p>

  <h2>What answers the question</h2>
  <p>Under the hood is Jev, a System One model from TypeSafe. It answers in one step, with no reasoning text and nothing to train. Ask a question it has never seen, in plain words, and it returns a probability it means. About a penny buys a thousand answers. <a href="/backends/">The backends page</a> says how to name a model and what to do when a new one ships.</p>

  <h2>Things you can ship this afternoon</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {sixHowtos.map((h) => (
      <a class="tile" href={`/how-tos/${h.slug}/`}>
        <b>{h.title}</b>
        <span>{h.reader}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/how-tos/">All eleven how-tos</a> · <a href="/recipes/">Shell recipes</a></p>

  <h2>Why a careful engineer trusts it</h2>
  <p>A question is a small file. It lives in your repo, diffs in a pull request, and runs against your own labeled cases before you trust it. Every answer carries the probability the model reported, and a band sends the middle to a person. The evidence crosses whole, answers are saved, and nothing else leaves the machine. The <a href="/trust/">trust page</a> lays out the outcomes, the thresholds, the cache, and how to check a question against your own labeled records.</p>
</Base>
